        <h1>Learning Rust while the agents write it</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          I picked Rust for LightPhotos without knowing Rust, and I still write
          very little of it. The planning, testing, review and release loop I
          run instead, and the four ways it goes wrong.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 8 min read</p>
      </header>

      <h2>Two problems I took on at once</h2>
      <p>
        A few days ago I wrote about <a href="/blogs/why-rust.html">choosing
        Rust for the whole app</a>. What I skipped in that post is that I didn't
        know Rust when I chose it. I had read about it. I had never shipped a
        line of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second problem is the goal itself. I don't want a photo editor that
        merely works. I want one where a slider moves and the image has already
        changed, on a folder with thousands of RAW files in it. That's a
        performance target, and performance is the one thing you can't add at
        the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        Taking on both at once is normally how a side project dies. What made
        it possible is that I'm not the one typing. Coding agents write
        essentially all of the Rust in LightPhotos. My job is the loop around
        them. Plan the change, get it built, prove it works, review it, ship it.
      </p>

      <h2>Where I actually spend my time</h2>

