      <h2>Planning is the part I won't hand over</h2>
      <p>
        Every change starts as a short written plan, and I write it. Not a
        document, not a spec with headings. Usually a paragraph and a list. It
        says what the change is, which parts of the app it touches, what is
        deliberately out of scope, and how I'll know it worked.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last item does most of the work. If I can't say what would prove
        the change is done, I don't have a plan yet, I have a wish. "Make
        thumbnails faster" is a wish. "A folder of 3,000 photos fills the first
        two screens of thumbnails without the scroll stuttering, and memory does
        not climb while I scroll" is a plan.
      </p>
      <p>
        Planning first is also the cheapest place to be wrong. An agent will
        happily build something confidently and completely in the wrong shape.
        Catching that in a paragraph costs me a minute. Catching it in a branch
        costs an afternoon, and I probably won't throw the branch away, because
        by then it compiles and I'm attached to it.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        The agent's wrong assumption is free while it's still a sentence. Once
        it's code that compiles, I start defending it.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        This is the same discipline as the seven-item list in
        <a href="/blogs/why-i-started-lightphotos.html">the first post</a>. It
        turns out that writing the list up front matters more, not less, when
        something can implement the list in twenty minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>Building one thing at a time</h2>
      <p>
        The rule I keep coming back to is that a change has to end somewhere I
        can stand. One task, then build, then run the app, then the next task.
        Not five tasks and a hopeful build at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        The reason isn't tidiness. When two changes land together and the app
        misbehaves, I've lost the one piece of information I care about, which
        is which change did it. Agents are very good at producing a lot of
        plausible code quickly, which means they're very good at destroying
        that information if I let them.
      </p>

      <h2>The agent cannot be the only witness</h2>
      <p>
        Agents are excellent at announcing success. "The thumbnail loading
        should now be much faster." Should. That sentence has cost me more time
        than any bug in the codebase.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the pixel maths has real tests, because it's the easy case. Exposure,
        the tone curve, white balance and the rest are pure functions, and a
        pure function is a thing you can pin down with numbers that don't move.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else I check by running the app myself, which is where I'm
        currently weakest. Scrolling, decoding, panning, memory. Clicking around
        in a window tells me whether something is broken. It doesn't reliably
        tell me whether a change made the app faster or slower, because I never
        click the same way twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don't have a good answer for that yet. It's the next thing I want to
        fix, and I expect it to be its own post. In the meantime my working rule
        is that it works when I ran it, not when the agent says so.
      </p>

      <h2>A reader that didn't write the code</h2>
      <p>
        When a change is done, a fresh agent reviews it. One with no memory of
        the conversation that produced it, told to go find what's wrong with the
        diff. That framing matters. The agent that just wrote four hundred lines
        is the worst possible judge of them, and it will agree with you
        enthusiastically either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        The recurring findings are boringly consistent, and they're almost all
        about waste rather than correctness.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Cloning to get past the borrow checker.</strong> The easiest
          way to make Rust stop complaining is to copy the data. On a 60 MB
          image buffer, in a function that runs per frame, that's a disaster
          that compiles cleanly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Allocating inside a per-pixel loop.</strong> Perfectly
          reasonable-looking code, run twenty million times.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>An <code>unwrap</code> on something a user controls,</strong>
          like a file that turned out not to be an image.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A lock held while slow work happens.</strong> This is the one
          that turns a background thread into a stalled UI.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Unasked-for refactors.</strong> Three files renamed, for
          reasons nobody requested.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Then I read the diff myself. Not to catch what the reviewer missed,
        because mostly I can't. I do it because this turned out to be how I
        learned Rust.
      </p>

      <h2>Making releases boring on purpose</h2>
      <p>
        A release is a version bump, a build for each target, and the site
        updated with the new build. It's scripted, and it's safe to run twice.
        That second property is the one I keep deliberately. A release I have to
        concentrate on is a release I'll put off, and then I'm sitting on a
        month of unshipped work.
      </p>

