      <h2>Four ways this loop goes wrong</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Whack-a-mole.</strong> A symptom gets fixed, another appears,
        and the agent cheerfully fixes that one too. Four rounds in, the code is
        a pile of guards and nobody has said out loud what is actually wrong.
        The only thing that works is to stop, reproduce the problem, and make
        the agent state the cause before it's allowed to touch a line.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Confident numbers that were never measured.</strong> I've been
        told a change made something "roughly three times faster" by an agent
        that ran nothing at all. Any number I didn't watch get produced is a
        guess wearing a number's clothes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Code that is correct and wasteful.</strong> Rust's compiler is a
        strict reviewer, and it made me braver than I should have been. It
        checks that you aren't doing anything unsafe. It has no opinion about
        whether you just copied fifty megabytes for no reason. For an app whose
        whole premise is speed, that's the gap I have to cover myself, and it's
        why performance has to be a gate in the loop rather than something I
        hope for.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Scope drift.</strong> Left unattended, a change grows. Naming
        what's out of scope in the plan handles most of it, and reading the diff
        handles the rest.
      </p>

      <h2>So how did I learn Rust?</h2>
      <p>
        Not from a book, and not by writing an app from scratch. Four things did
        it, in rough order of how much they taught me.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Reading diffs with one question.</strong> Why is this line
          here? Repeated a few hundred times across real code that does
          something I care about.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Asking for justifications.</strong> Why a clone here, why a
          reference there, why <code>Arc</code> and not a plain value. The
          answers are where ownership stopped being a rule I'd read about and
          became something I could predict.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Borrow checker errors.</strong> A tutor with no patience and
          no small talk, which turns out to be an effective combination.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writing some of it by hand anyway.</strong> A function per
          session, no agent. Slow, and the only way I found out what I actually
          understood.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A few weeks in, I can read all of the Rust in LightPhotos and write most
        of it. Reading is the half that matters for this loop. An agent can
        always produce more code than I can write, but it can't give me the
        judgment to tell whether the code it produced is any good, and that's
        the only thing standing between "it works" and the app I'm trying to
        build.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write the plan, including the proof.</strong> If I can't say
          what would show the change worked, the agent will pick its own
          definition of done, and it will be generous.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Be the witness.</strong> "Should now be faster" is not a
          result. A number I watched get produced is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Review with a reader that has no history.</strong> Whatever
          wrote the code is the worst judge of it, and the most agreeable.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One change at a time.</strong> Cheap to do, and it preserves
          the only clue worth having when something breaks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Learn the language anyway.</strong> Not to write it faster,
          but to be able to tell good from plausible.
        </li>
      </ul>
